## Approach

Create a single integration test file `tests/parse_python_test.rs` following the existing adapter test pattern (cf. `parse_c_test.rs`, `parse-dart-test.rs`). Single-file tests use `parse_file()` for fast symbol/import/attr assertions. Multi-file tests use `tempfile::tempdir()` + `parse_workspace()` for import resolution, effect detection, and status verification. Effect detection requires the full pipeline because `enrich_all_effects` runs inside `conventions::pipeline::rebuild`, which is called by `post_parse_sequence` at the end of `parse_workspace`.

Alternative considered: testing effects via direct `enrich_all_effects` calls with mock `RefRow`s. Rejected because the acceptance criterion says "end-to-end" and the unresolved-name fallback path depends on how the parser produces refs and how they're stored in the DB — mocking would skip that chain.

## Files

- **Create**: `tests/parse_python_test.rs` — all Python adapter integration tests
- **Modify**: none expected (all necessary types/functions are already `pub`)

## Steps

### Step 1: Scaffold test file with shared imports and helpers

Set up the test file with common imports and a `make_python_entry` helper that creates a `WorkspaceEntry` with `languages: vec!["python"]`.

```rust
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;

use sutra::config::Config;
use sutra::db::Db;
use sutra::parser::adapter::default_registry;
use sutra::parser::{flatten_symbols, parse_file, RefContextKind, SymbolKind};
use sutra::pipeline;
use sutra::workspace::WorkspaceEntry;

fn make_config(db_dir: &std::path::Path) -> Config {
    Config {
        db_dir: db_dir.to_path_buf(),
        workspaces_path: db_dir.join("workspaces.toml"),
        listen_addr: "127.0.0.1:0".to_string(),
        parse_parallelism: 1,
        stale_threshold_sec: 600,
        log_level: "warn".to_string(),
        constraints_idle_timeout_sec: 1800,
        parse_timeout_ms: 5000,
    }
}

fn make_python_entry(id: &str, root: PathBuf) -> WorkspaceEntry {
    WorkspaceEntry {
        id: id.to_string(),
        root,
        languages: vec!["python".to_string()],
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Symbol extraction smoke tests

Test function, class with methods, module-level constants/statics, visibility rules, and qualified names.

```rust
#[test]
fn smoke_function() {
    let r = parse_file("def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n", "python", "math.py").unwrap();
    assert!(r.parsed_ok);
    assert_eq!(r.symbols.len(), 1);
    let sym = &r.symbols[0];
    assert_eq!(sym.kind, SymbolKind::Function);
    assert_eq!(sym.qualified_name, "add");
    assert!(sym.signature.as_ref().unwrap().contains("def add"));
    assert_eq!(sym.visibility.as_deref(), Some("pub"));
}

#[test]
fn class_with_methods() {
    let src = "class Greeter:\n    def hello(self):\n        pass\n    def _internal(self):\n        pass\n";
    let r = parse_file(src, "python", "greet.py").unwrap();
    let flat = flatten_symbols(&r.symbols);
    let cls = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "Greeter").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(cls.kind, SymbolKind::Class);
    assert_eq!(cls.visibility.as_deref(), Some("pub"));
    let hello = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "hello").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(hello.kind, SymbolKind::Method);
    assert_eq!(hello.qualified_name, "Greeter::hello");
    let internal = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "_internal").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(internal.visibility.as_deref(), Some("private"));
}

#[test]
fn module_level_constants_and_statics() {
    let src = "MAX_SIZE = 100\ndefault_name = 'world'\n";
    let r = parse_file(src, "python", "config.py").unwrap();
    let max = r.symbols.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "MAX_SIZE").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(max.kind, SymbolKind::Const);
    let default = r.symbols.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "default_name").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(default.kind, SymbolKind::Static);
}
```

### Step 3: Import extraction tests

Cover `import X`, `from X import Y`, relative imports, aliases, and wildcard.

```rust
#[test]
fn import_extraction() {
    let src = "import os\nimport os.path\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom . import sibling\nfrom pkg.sub import foo, bar\nimport sys as system\nfrom mod import *\n";
    let r = parse_file(src, "python", "imports.py").unwrap();
    let paths: Vec<&str> = r.imports.iter().map(|i| i.raw_path.as_str()).collect();
    assert!(paths.contains(&"os"));
    assert!(paths.contains(&"os.path"));
    assert!(paths.contains(&"pathlib.Path"));
    assert!(paths.contains(&".sibling"));
    assert!(paths.contains(&"pkg.sub.foo"));
    assert!(paths.contains(&"pkg.sub.bar"));
    assert!(paths.contains(&"sys"));       // alias dropped, original kept
    assert!(paths.contains(&"mod"));       // wildcard → just module
    // Check kind field
    let os_imp = r.imports.iter().find(|i| i.raw_path == "os").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(os_imp.kind, "import");
    let path_imp = r.imports.iter().find(|i| i.raw_path == "pathlib.Path").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(path_imp.kind, "from_import");
}
```

### Step 4: Decorator / language_attrs tests

Test `@staticmethod`, `@property`, `@pytest.fixture` produce correct `decorator:X` attributes plus `has_decorator` flag.

```rust
#[test]
fn decorator_attrs() {
    let src = r#"
class Foo:
    @staticmethod
    def create():
        pass

    @property
    def name(self):
        return self._name

import pytest

@pytest.fixture
def db_conn():
    return connect()
"#;
    let r = parse_file(src, "python", "decorators.py").unwrap();
    let flat = flatten_symbols(&r.symbols);

    let create = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "create").unwrap();
    let attrs: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(create.language_attrs.as_deref().unwrap()).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(attrs["has_decorator"], true);
    assert_eq!(attrs["decorator:staticmethod"], true);

    let name = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "name").unwrap();
    let attrs: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(name.language_attrs.as_deref().unwrap()).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(attrs["decorator:property"], true);

    let db_conn = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "db_conn").unwrap();
    let attrs: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(db_conn.language_attrs.as_deref().unwrap()).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(attrs["decorator:pytest.fixture"], true);
}
```

### Step 5: Async generator test

Verify `async def` with `yield` produces both `is_async` and `is_generator` in `language_attrs`.

```rust
#[test]
fn async_generator_attrs() {
    let src = "async def stream(items):\n    for item in items:\n        yield item\n";
    let r = parse_file(src, "python", "stream.py").unwrap();
    let sym = &r.symbols[0];
    let attrs: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_str(sym.language_attrs.as_deref().unwrap()).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(attrs["is_async"], true, "should be async");
    assert_eq!(attrs["is_generator"], true, "should be generator");
}
```

### Step 6: Complexity scoring test

Write a function with nested `if`/`for`/`try`/comprehension and verify `cyclomatic`, `cognitive`, and `max_nesting` values on the symbol.

```rust
#[test]
fn complexity_scoring() {
    let src = r#"
def process(items):
    results = []
    for item in items:
        if item.valid:
            try:
                results.append(item.transform())
            except ValueError:
                pass
    filtered = [r for r in results if r is not None]
    return filtered
"#;
    let r = parse_file(src, "python", "complex.py").unwrap();
    let sym = &r.symbols[0];
    // for + if + try/except + comprehension-if + list_comprehension
    // Cyclomatic: 1 + for(1) + if(1) + except(1) + list_comp(1) = 5 minimum
    assert!(sym.cyclomatic.unwrap() >= 5, "cyclomatic={}", sym.cyclomatic.unwrap());
    // Cognitive: for(1+0) + if(1+1) + try(1+2) + except(+1) + comp(+1) = significant
    assert!(sym.cognitive.unwrap() > 0, "cognitive should be non-zero");
    // max_nesting: for > if > try = depth 3
    assert!(sym.max_nesting.unwrap() >= 3, "max_nesting={}", sym.max_nesting.unwrap());
}
```

### Step 7: TestCase detection test

Verify that a class inheriting from `unittest.TestCase` gets `FLAG_TEST`, and that `test_` prefixed functions also get it.

```rust
#[test]
fn testcase_detection() {
    let src = r#"
import unittest

class TestMath(unittest.TestCase):
    def test_add(self):
        self.assertEqual(1 + 1, 2)

    def helper(self):
        pass

def test_standalone():
    assert True

def regular_function():
    pass
"#;
    let r = parse_file(src, "python", "test_math.py").unwrap();
    let flat = flatten_symbols(&r.symbols);

    let test_class = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "TestMath").unwrap();
    assert_ne!(test_class.flags & 0x01, 0, "TestCase subclass should have FLAG_TEST");

    let test_add = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "test_add").unwrap();
    assert_ne!(test_add.flags & 0x01, 0, "test_ method should have FLAG_TEST");

    let test_standalone = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "test_standalone").unwrap();
    assert_ne!(test_standalone.flags & 0x01, 0, "test_ function should have FLAG_TEST");

    let regular = flat.iter().find(|s| s.short_name == "regular_function").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(regular.flags & 0x01, 0, "regular function should NOT have FLAG_TEST");
}
```

### Step 8: Multi-file pipeline test — import resolution + deps edges

Write two Python files to a tempdir with cross-file imports. Run `parse_workspace`, verify `snap.files_parsed == 2`, then query `db.import_edges()` to verify resolved file→file edges.

```rust
#[test]
fn cross_file_import_resolution() {
    let dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();
    // Package structure: mypackage/__init__.py, mypackage/models.py, mypackage/app.py
    let pkg = dir.path().join("mypackage");
    std::fs::create_dir_all(&pkg).unwrap();
    std::fs::write(pkg.join("__init__.py"), "").unwrap();
    std::fs::write(pkg.join("models.py"), "class User:\n    pass\n\nclass Post:\n    pass\n").unwrap();
    std::fs::write(pkg.join("app.py"), "from mypackage.models import User\n\ndef handler(u: User):\n    pass\n").unwrap();

    let db_dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();
    let ws = make_python_entry("py-imports", dir.path().to_path_buf());
    let config = make_config(db_dir.path());
    let db = Db::open_unchecked(&ws.id, db_dir.path()).unwrap();
    let cancel = AtomicBool::new(false);
    let registry = default_registry();

    let snap = pipeline::parse_workspace(&ws, &db, &config, &cancel, &registry).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(snap.files_parsed, 3); // __init__.py, models.py, app.py

    let edges = db.import_edges().unwrap();
    let app_file = db.file_by_path("mypackage/app.py").unwrap().unwrap();
    let models_file = db.file_by_path("mypackage/models.py").unwrap().unwrap();
    assert!(
        edges.iter().any(|&(from, to)| from == app_file.id && to == models_file.id),
        "app.py should have a resolved import edge to models.py"
    );
}
```

### Step 9: Effect detection end-to-end test

Write a Python file with functions calling `open()`, `requests.get()`, `subprocess.run()`. Run `parse_workspace` (which runs the full conventions pipeline including `enrich_all_effects`). Then manually call `extract_attrs_for_symbol` + `enrich_all_effects` on the DB symbols/refs to verify effect attributes — this mirrors exactly what `conventions::pipeline::rebuild` does, using the same DB-stored refs.

```rust
#[test]
fn effect_detection_end_to_end() {
    let dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();
    std::fs::write(dir.path().join("effects.py"), r#"
def read_file(path):
    f = open(path)
    return f.read()

def fetch_url(url):
    import requests
    return requests.get(url)

def run_cmd(cmd):
    import subprocess
    return subprocess.run(cmd)
"#).unwrap();

    let db_dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();
    let ws = make_python_entry("py-effects", dir.path().to_path_buf());
    let config = make_config(db_dir.path());
    let db = Db::open_unchecked(&ws.id, db_dir.path()).unwrap();
    let cancel = AtomicBool::new(false);
    let registry = default_registry();

    pipeline::parse_workspace(&ws, &db, &config, &cancel, &registry).unwrap();

    let file = db.file_by_path("effects.py").unwrap().unwrap();
    let syms = db.find_symbols_by_file(file.id).unwrap();
    let refs = db.find_refs_in_file(file.id).unwrap();
    let adapter = registry.adapter_for_language("python").unwrap();
    let fca_source = adapter.as_fca_source().unwrap();
    let callee_cache = std::collections::HashMap::new(); // no resolved callees for stdlib

    for (fn_name, expected_effect) in [("read_file", "effect:fs"), ("fetch_url", "effect:net"), ("run_cmd", "effect:process")] {
        let sym = syms.iter().find(|s| &*s.short_name == fn_name)
            .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!("missing symbol {fn_name}"));
        let mut attrs = sutra::conventions::extract_attrs_for_symbol(sym, "effects.py", "python", &registry)
            .unwrap_or_else(|| panic!("no attrs for {fn_name}"));
        sutra::conventions::enrich_all_effects(&mut attrs, sym, &refs, &callee_cache, fca_source, None);
        assert!(
            attrs.attributes.contains(&expected_effect.to_string()),
            "{fn_name} should have {expected_effect}, got: {:?}", attrs.attributes
        );
    }
}
```

Note: the `sutra::conventions` module path for `extract_attrs_for_symbol` and `enrich_all_effects` must be verified — they may live under `sutra::conventions::attributes` depending on the module re-export structure. Check `src/conventions/mod.rs` re-exports. If not re-exported, either use the full path (`sutra::conventions::attributes::*`) or add re-exports.

### Step 10: Python adapter registration and effect patterns test

Verify the adapter is in the registry, returns correct extensions, and has the expected effect patterns.

```rust
#[test]
fn python_adapter_registered() {
    let registry = default_registry();
    let adapter = registry.adapter_for_language("python").unwrap();
    assert_eq!(adapter.language_id(), "python");
    assert!(adapter.extensions().contains(&"py"));
    let fca = adapter.as_fca_source().expect("Python should have FCA source");
    let effect_names: Vec<_> = fca.effect_patterns().iter().map(|p| p.attr_name).collect();
    assert!(effect_names.contains(&"effect:fs"));
    assert!(effect_names.contains(&"effect:net"));
    assert!(effect_names.contains(&"effect:db"));
    assert!(effect_names.contains(&"effect:io"));
    assert!(effect_names.contains(&"effect:process"));
}
```

### Step 11: Status verification — Python files indexed

After running `parse_workspace` with `.py` files, verify `snap.files_parsed` includes them and `db.all_files()` has the Python files with `language == "python"`.

```rust
#[test]
fn python_files_indexed_in_status() {
    let dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();
    std::fs::write(dir.path().join("app.py"), "def main():\n    pass\n").unwrap();
    std::fs::write(dir.path().join("utils.py"), "def helper():\n    pass\n").unwrap();

    let db_dir = tempfile::tempdir().unwrap();
    let ws = make_python_entry("py-status", dir.path().to_path_buf());
    let config = make_config(db_dir.path());
    let db = Db::open_unchecked(&ws.id, db_dir.path()).unwrap();
    let cancel = AtomicBool::new(false);
    let registry = default_registry();

    let snap = pipeline::parse_workspace(&ws, &db, &config, &cancel, &registry).unwrap();
    assert_eq!(snap.files_parsed, 2);

    let files = db.all_files().unwrap();
    let py_files: Vec<_> = files.iter().filter(|f| f.language == "python").collect();
    assert_eq!(py_files.len(), 2);
}
```

